    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ceefe053_14739514",
        "filename": "board/hyperdebug/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 353,
      "author": {
        "id": 1341996
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-14T18:54:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The hardware supports up to 12 bytes being sent in the initial phase of an \"indirect read\" operation.  For my use case, I needed at most 5 bytes, so I implemented up to 8.  I have now added handling of up to 12 bytes, for good measure.",
      "parentUuid": "920a5860_6b6fbb47",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 353,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 353,
        "endChar": 30
      },
      "revId": "54f04776aed600406b45bae45add46ce3f07ab93",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8dc10600_cbbc8c79",
        "filename": "board/hyperdebug/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 367,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-13T20:08:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If txlen \u003c\u003d 8, txdata is handled as instructions and if txlen \u003e 8, txdata is handled as raw data?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 358,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 367,
        "endChar": 39
      },
      "revId": "54f04776aed600406b45bae45add46ce3f07ab93",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fd2a5cb5_a73064c5",
        "filename": "board/hyperdebug/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 367,
      "author": {
        "id": 1341996
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-14T18:54:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, this OCTOSPI controller is designed to communicate with SPI flash chips efficiently.  Often, when you want to read a kilobyte of data, you do so by sending 5-6 bytes of instruction+address, and then read the data.  Because of that, the controller has special support for sending a few bytes before reading a lot.  I want to make use of that, in the common case that the USB host requests a transaction consisting of a few bytes to write followed by a lot to read.",
      "parentUuid": "8dc10600_cbbc8c79",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 358,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 367,
        "endChar": 39
      },
      "revId": "54f04776aed600406b45bae45add46ce3f07ab93",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
